          question of parts, manuals and sensible limits rather than of buying new, and the LS10.0T —
          the model most people ask about — is better supported for parts than most discontinued
          treadmills of its age.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is the LIVESTRONG Treadmill Still Sold? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>No — not new in the US. LIVESTRONG treadmills were made by Johnson Health Tech
            under a licence that began in 2009, and Johnson no longer sells exercise equipment under
            the LIVESTRONG name.</strong> The LS10.0T, the mid-range model in the line, is now a used
            machine: there is no manufacturer stock, and any original warranty has long expired.
          </p>
          <p>
            That does not make a LIVESTRONG treadmill a bad machine to own. Johnson's own owner's
            manual rates the LS10.0T for users up to 325 lb and gave it a lifetime frame and motor
            warranty when new, which tells you the company built it to a reasonable standard. And
            unusually for a discontinued treadmill, most of its parts are still listed: on 14 October
            2026 a large US parts supplier showed 189 catalogued parts for the LS10.0T, of which only
            five were marked no longer available.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the practical advice is straightforward. If you own an LS10.0T that runs well, keep it
            lubricated and keep using it. If it has a fault, identify the exact model version before
            buying anything, because there are four. If you are thinking of buying one used, pay a
            used-treadmill price, see it running first, and read our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>.
            And if you are looking for a new machine in the same spirit, the closest thing is
            Johnson's own Horizon range, which we cover at the end.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-made-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Made LIVESTRONG Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            Johnson Health Tech North America, the US arm of the Taiwanese manufacturer Johnson
            Health Tech, made them under licence. Two trade reports from 2009 agree on the outline.
            Johnson received exclusive rights to use the LIVESTRONG name on consumer and commercial
            fitness equipment in the spring of 2009, under a multi-year agreement that committed a
            minimum of $4 million to the Lance Armstrong Foundation, the cancer charity behind the
            LIVESTRONG name. The first products were treadmills, ellipticals and an indoor cycle.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where it was sold</h3>
          <p>
            Dick's Sporting Goods was the exclusive US retailer at launch, and the machines were in
            its stores by October 2009, with four treadmills in the first range. In Canada, Canadian
            Tire announced a premium LIVESTRONG range in January 2012, including an LSPro2 treadmill,
            again manufactured by Johnson. That is why you will find LIVESTRONG treadmills with
            Canadian part suffixes as well as US ones.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why the name disappeared</h3>
          <p>
            We found no announcement of the licence ending, so we cannot give a date. What we can
            say is that independent review sites describe the line as discontinued: one that reviewed
            the ellipticals in 2012 already described models as soon to be unavailable in the US, and
            another states that Johnson no longer sells equipment under the LIVESTRONG name, with
            some models lingering in Canada for a time. The charity's founder, Lance Armstrong, was later
            stripped of his Tour de France titles in a doping scandal, but we have not found a source
            tying the end of the treadmill line to it, and it does not matter to an owner.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What it means today</h3>
          <p>
            The reason it is worth knowing who made them is that Johnson Health Tech still exists and
            still makes treadmills, under brands including Horizon, Matrix and, since it bought the
            brand in 2024, Bowflex. The LIVESTRONG machines share engineering habits and suppliers
            with Johnson's other products of the same era, which is part of why parts are still
            catalogued. It does not mean Johnson's current brands will service a LIVESTRONG treadmill
            under warranty. Our
            <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex brand page</a>
            covers that 2024 acquisition.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more thing to separate out: LIVESTRONG.COM, the health and fitness website, is not a
            treadmill maker. The LS10.0T's online workout-journal feature was built to sync with it,
            which is why the two names appear together in old reviews, but the website has never
            supported the machine as a product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ls10-0t" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The LIVESTRONG LS10.0T: What Is Confirmed</h2>
<p>
            The LS10.0T sat in the middle of the US range, between the LS8.0T and the LS13.0T, with
            the LS15.0T above. Its owner's manual is copyright 2010, and the largest parts supplier
            files the main version as a 2011 model. Here is what we could confirm, and what we could
            not.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Item</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Figure</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Maximum user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">325 lb (147 kg)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirmed: Johnson manual and an independent review</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty when new</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime frame and motor, 3 years parts, 1 year labour</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Confirmed, and now expired in practice</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Electrical supply</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Minimum 15-amp circuit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">From the manual</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor rating</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.75 CHP in one review; 2.50 CHP on a later Canadian LS10T listing</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Conflicting</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running area</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 55 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">One review only</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed and incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">To 12 mph and 12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">One review only</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Review, consistent with the parts catalogue</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The warranty in the manual is worth reading closely. It covered the frame and the drive
            and elevation motors for life, for the original owner only, with three years on
            electronics and parts and one year on labour. The LS13.0T above it was rated for 350 lb,
            with five years on parts and two on labour. Because the lifetime cover was limited to the
            original owner, and because the brand has been withdrawn, a used buyer should treat an
            LS10.0T as having no warranty at all.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The console</h3>
          <p>
            The manual describes an LCD window showing time, distance, speed, calories, incline,
            laps and heart rate from grip sensors; dedicated speed and incline keys plus quick keys
            for jumping to a set value; an audio input with built-in speakers; a personal fan; an
            energy-saver standby mode; a USB port used for software updates; a safety key; and the
            LiveTrack fitness journal, which stored accumulated workout data. Treat the online side
            of LiveTrack as historical: we found no current support for it, and none of the basic
            functions depend on it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Which version do you have?</h3>
          <p>
            Parts catalogues list four variants under the LS10.0T name, and the code that tells them
            apart is the TM number on the serial sticker: TM406 (the main US model), TM406B, TM438
            (listed as LS10.T-02) and TM438C (listed as LS10.0T-C2, a Canadian version). The manual
            puts the serial number on a white barcode sticker near the power switch and cord.
            Photograph it before you order anything; some parts are shared across all four, others
            are not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Can You Still Get LIVESTRONG LS10.0T Parts?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, mostly — which is better news than most owners of discontinued treadmills get.
            FitnessRepairParts.com, a large US parts supplier, catalogues the LS10.0T (TM406) with
            exploded diagrams for the console masts, side rails, motor set and the rest of the unit.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the catalogue showed on 14 October 2026</h3>
          <p>
            Of 189 catalogued parts, five were marked no longer available: the speed sensor, the
            incline motor, the keypad overlay, an optical disc and a washer. Two were on extended back
            order, including the drive motor. Everything else carried a price. Typical examples:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Part</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Listed price</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Note</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking belt (treadbelt)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$125.13</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">In stock; fits TM406 and TM406B</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$29.10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$129.98</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Front and rear rollers</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$176.54 and $90.21</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor controller (110 V)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$428.16</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Plus a $60 refundable core charge</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$534.71</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Extended back order</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key, lubricant, owner's manual</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$14.55, $15.76, $13.10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed sensor, incline motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">—</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No longer available</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Prices are those shown on that date and will change. The pattern matters more than the
            numbers: wear parts are cheap and easy, the big electrical parts are available but
            expensive enough to make you think, and two of the parts that commonly fail on any
            treadmill — the speed sensor and the incline motor — are the ones that have run out.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When a part has gone</h3>
          <p>
            A missing speed sensor or incline motor is not automatically the end. A speed sensor is
            usually a simple magnetic pickup, and an independent technician may be able to fit a
            compatible one; our
            <a href="/treadmill-speed-sensor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">speed sensor guide</a>
            explains how they work and fail. A dead incline motor can sometimes be sourced used, or
            the machine can be run flat. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>
            covers when a technician makes sense and what jobs typically cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="manuals-and-maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Finding the LS10.0T Manual, and What It Tells You</h2>
<p>
            Johnson's manuals for the LS10.0T survive on public manual archives. ManualsLib holds an
            80-page owner's manual covering the LS10.0T and LS13.0T, a shorter console guide, a
            122-page combined manual for the LS10.0T, LS13.0T and LS15.0T, and service manuals for
            the LS10.0T and the LS10.0T-02. The parts supplier above also lists a printed owner's
            manual as an orderable part.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The LUBE message</h3>
          <p>
            The service manual explains that the console shows a "LUBE" message after 150 miles
            (240 km), as a reminder that the deck needs lubricating. It is a maintenance prompt, not
            a fault. Lubricate with the type the manual specifies — the parts catalogue lists a
            Johnson lubricant for this model — and clear the reminder as the manual describes. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>
            gives the general method. Unplug the treadmill before you lift the belt or remove the
            motor hood, every time.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The E1 code</h3>
          <p>
            The same service manual says an E1 shown during the console's auto-calibration points to
            the speed sensor wire, which runs from the left of the frame near the front roller pulley
            to the motor control board. Check that the connector is seated and the wire is intact
            before assuming the sensor itself has failed — the sensor is one of the parts no longer
            listed, so ruling out the wire first is worth the effort.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The power supply</h3>
          <p>
            The owner's manual asks for a minimum 15-amp circuit. A treadmill that trips breakers or
            behaves erratically on a shared circuit or an extension lead is often fine on a circuit
            of its own; our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cords and dedicated circuits</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Should You Buy a Used LIVESTRONG Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            It can be a reasonable buy for walking and jogging, if the price reflects its age and
            you see it running first. The LS10.0T was a mid-range machine from a serious
            manufacturer, it is rated for heavier users than many budget treadmills, and its parts
            are better catalogued than most. The risks are age and the specific parts that have run
            out.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What it is worth</h3>
          <p>
            We do not have resale data, so treat this as a rough estimate. One review site gives
            the LS10.0T's original price as about $999. A treadmill that is now well over a decade
            old, with no warranty, is worth what it will save you over a new machine in the next few
            years of use. As a rule of thumb, a working LS10.0T in good condition is a low-hundreds
            purchase, and less if the belt or deck is worn — a new walking belt and deck together
            cost over $250 in parts before any labour. Collection is part of the cost: it is a heavy
            folding treadmill, and moving it safely takes two people. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving a treadmill</a>
            covers how.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to test one</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Read the serial sticker and note the TM number, then check the parts catalogue for
            that variant on your phone before you pay.</li>
            <li>Run it for at least ten minutes across its speed range. Listen for grinding,
            knocking or a rising whine, and watch for an E1 or other code on start-up.</li>
            <li>Take it through the whole incline range and back. The incline motor is no longer
            listed, so a slow or stalling incline is a real problem, not a minor one.</li>
            <li>Lift the belt edge and feel the deck. A dry, rough or worn deck adds load to the
            motor and controller.</li>
            <li>Check the console: all keys, the grip pulse sensors and the safety key. The keypad
            overlay is no longer available, so cracked or dead keys stay that way.</li>
            <li>Fold and unfold it, and check that the deck lowers smoothly and locks.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to where to buy a used treadmill</a>
            covers the safest places to look, and our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used comparison</a>
            helps you decide whether the saving is worth it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Repair or Replace an Old LIVESTRONG?</h2>
<p>
            It depends on what has failed. The table gives our general view for the LS10.0T based on
            what the parts catalogue showed; it is editorial judgment, not a rule.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Fault</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Part status</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Our general view</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt slipping or worn</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt, drive belt and lubricant listed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Noisy or seized roller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Both rollers listed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No power, cuts out, erratic speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controller listed at over $400</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Diagnose first; repair only if the rest is sound</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor failed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listed, on extended back order</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually replace the treadmill</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline not working</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline motor no longer available</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Source used, or use it flat</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E1 or speed reading wrong</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wire listed; sensor no longer available</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the wire, then ask a technician</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The rule we use for any old treadmill is to stop spending once a single repair approaches
            a large share of what a reliable modern replacement costs. On an LS10.0T, the controller
            and motor are the two parts that cross that line. Our guides to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            and the
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            help you narrow down a fault before you pay anyone.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Safety and recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database on 14 October 2026.
            It lists one LIVESTRONG recall, from January 2010, and it is for ellipticals, not
            treadmills: Johnson recalled about 18,000 Horizon and LIVESTRONG elliptical trainers,
            including the LIVESTRONG LS7.9E, LS9.9E and LS12.9E, because a foot pedal could
            disengage, and offered free in-home replacement of the pedals. We found no recall of a
            LIVESTRONG treadmill. That does not make an old machine safe by default: stop using any
            treadmill that smells of burning, trips a breaker, changes speed on its own or will not
            stop, and make sure the safety key still cuts the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-replaced-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Replaced the LIVESTRONG Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            No model was announced as the LS10.0T's successor, so there is no direct replacement to
            name. The nearest thing in spirit is Johnson Health Tech's own Horizon range: the same
            manufacturer, sold in the US now, with current warranties and parts support. Horizon's
            current folding machines sit at a similar point in the market to where the LIVESTRONG
            line did.
          </p>
          <p>
            If what you liked about the LS10.0T was a sturdy folding treadmill rated for heavier
            users with a 12 mph top speed, the Horizon 7.0 AT is the closest match on this site: our
            roundup of the
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best Horizon treadmills</a>
            lists it with a 20 by 60-inch deck, 0.5 to 12 mph, incline to 15% and a 325 lb rating —
            a longer deck than the LS10.0T was reported to have, at the same confirmed user weight.
            Our page asking
            <a href="/is-horizon-a-good-treadmill-brand/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether Horizon is a good brand</a>
            covers its warranties and its 2022 recall of the T101-05, which was remedied.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want to look beyond Johnson, our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-brands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill brands guide</a>
            compares the companies making treadmills now, and our
            <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding treadmill picks</a>
            cover the format the LS10.0T used.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Getting rid of the old one</h3>
          <p>
            A working LIVESTRONG can go to a buyer, a charity or a friend; a dead one is heavy scrap.
            Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-dispose-of-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">disposing of a treadmill</a>
            covers the options, from municipal bulk collection to retailer haul-away when you buy a
            replacement.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who made LIVESTRONG treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Johnson Health Tech North America, part of the Taiwanese manufacturer Johnson Health Tech, which also makes Horizon and Matrix equipment and has owned Bowflex since 2024. It received exclusive rights to use the LIVESTRONG name on fitness equipment in 2009, with a minimum of $4 million committed to the Lance Armstrong Foundation. Dick's Sporting Goods was the launch retailer in the US.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the LIVESTRONG LS10.0T still made?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Johnson no longer sells exercise equipment under the LIVESTRONG name in the US, and the LS10.0T's owner's manual dates from 2010. It is now a used machine only. Any lifetime warranty applied to the original owner, so a second-hand buyer should assume there is no warranty at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on the LIVESTRONG LS10.0T?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                325 lb, or 147 kg, according to Johnson's own owner's manual, and an independent review gives the same figure. The LS13.0T above it was rated for 350 lb. As a rule of thumb, choose a treadmill rated comfortably above your body weight, especially on an older machine whose motor and deck have years of wear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I get LIVESTRONG LS10.0T parts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Specialist suppliers such as FitnessRepairParts.com catalogue the LS10.0T. On 14 October 2026 it listed 189 parts for the main TM406 version, with five marked no longer available, including the speed sensor and incline motor. Check the TM number on your serial sticker first, because four versions of the LS10.0T exist and not every part fits all of them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does LUBE mean on a LIVESTRONG treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a maintenance reminder, not a fault. The LS10.0T service manual says the console shows LUBE after 150 miles, or 240 kilometres, to prompt you to lubricate the deck. Unplug the treadmill, lubricate it with the type the manual specifies, then clear the reminder as the manual describes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a used LIVESTRONG treadmill worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, cheaply and after testing. The LS10.0T was a mid-range machine from a large manufacturer with good parts coverage, but it is now well over a decade old, has no warranty, and its incline motor and speed sensor are no longer listed. As a rough estimate, pay no more than the low hundreds for a good working example.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With a LIVESTRONG Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most problems with old LIVESTRONG machines come from ordering the wrong part or overpaying. These are the four to avoid.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering parts without the TM number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">There are four LS10.0T variants, from TM406 to TM438C, and parts catalogues list them separately. A walking belt that fits the TM406 and TM406B is not necessarily right for the others. Read the serial sticker near the power switch, photograph it, and search by the full variant before you buy.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying one that will not run through its incline range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The incline motor is no longer listed for the LS10.0T. A seller who says the incline just needs a reset may be right, but you cannot count on a replacement part. Insist on seeing the incline go fully up and down, and price the machine as a flat treadmill if it does not.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the lifetime warranty still applies</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The manual limited the lifetime frame and motor cover to the original owner, and the brand has since been withdrawn. Johnson's current brands will not treat a LIVESTRONG as their product under warranty. Budget for repairs as an owner of an unwarranted machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spending motor money on a decade-old treadmill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A new controller is listed at over $400 and a drive motor at over $500, before labour. Either can approach the price of a new budget treadmill with a warranty. Diagnose the fault first, decide your ceiling before you start, and stop when one repair would cost a large share of a replacement.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

